1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A greedy algorithm builds a solution one piece at a time, and at each step takes the piece that looks best right now.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The second half of the promise: the choice is never taken back. No backtracking, no trying the alternative.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Two properties are needed for a correct greedy algorithm: optimal substructure, and the greedy-choice property.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
So the exam question is never "is this greedy?" It is "is this greedy choice provably safe, and on which instances?"

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Making change: pay 6 units using as few coins as possible from the set 1, 3 and 4.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Greedy takes the largest coin that fits: 4, leaving 2. Then 1, then 1. Three coins.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Two coins of 3 also pay 6. The optimum is 2, so this greedy rule is not optimal on this set.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
With coins 1, 2 and 5 the same rule is optimal, and with 1, 3 and 4 it is not. Correctness is a property of the pair: rule and instance family.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Eleven activities, each an interval from a start time to a finish time. One room. Choose as many non-overlapping activities as possible.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Sort by finish time. That is the only preparation the algorithm needs.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Take the first activity, then walk right and keep an activity exactly when its start is at or after the last finish taken.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The greedy answer is a1, a4, a8, a11: four activities. An exhaustive search over all subsets confirms that four is the maximum.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Three criteria look reasonable: shortest activity first, earliest start first, earliest finish first. Only the third is safe.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Shortest first fails: with the long activities 0 to 5 and 5 to 10 plus a short one from 4 to 6, greedy takes the short one and stops at one activity.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Earliest start fails on the eleven-activity instance itself: activity a3 starts at 0 and runs to 6, and that single choice leaves only three activities where four are possible.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Earliest finish is safe because finishing early cannot reduce what remains. That sentence is the intuition; scene 5 turns it into an argument.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
An exchange argument compares the greedy solution with an arbitrary optimal one, and swaps one choice at a time until they agree.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Let the optimal solution be S star, and let g be the activity greedy takes first. If g is already in S star, there is nothing to do.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
If not, let s be the first activity of S star. Greedy chose g because g finishes no later than s. Replace s by g.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Now an optimal solution agrees with greedy on the first choice, so the remaining problem is the same shape and smaller. Induct on the number of activities.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Fractional knapsack: a bag of capacity 50, and three items whose fractions may be taken.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
The greedy criterion is value per unit weight: 6, 5 and 4. Sort descending, then fill.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Take I1 whole for 60 and 10 units of weight, I2 whole for 100 and 20 more. Capacity used is 30, so 20 remain.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
I3 does not fit whole, so take two thirds of it for 80. Total 240, and the exchange argument works because a fraction can always be trimmed.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Same bag, same capacity, same three items. One word changes: each item is taken whole or not at all.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Ratio order is still I1 then I2 then I3. Greedy takes I1 and I2 for 160, and I3 no longer fits.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
I2 and I3 together weigh exactly 50 and are worth 220. Greedy is off by 60.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The exchange step fails because a whole item cannot be trimmed to fill the gap. Divisibility was load-bearing.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Huffman coding: give frequent symbols short codes, and keep the code prefix-free so decoding is unambiguous.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Start with one leaf per symbol. Repeatedly merge the two lightest nodes into a parent whose weight is their sum.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Each merge adds one internal node. The total cost equals the sum of all internal node weights, so you can add the merges and never draw the tree.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
On frequencies 1, 2, 2, 2, 3 the merges cost 3, 4, 6 and 10, so the encoded message is 23 bits. Greedy, and optimal.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Question one: is there an exchange argument, or only a counterexample? Try to break the rule on a small instance first.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Question two: are the choices indivisible? If they are, suspect that greedy fails and that DP is needed.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Question three: does the problem have overlapping subproblems? If yes and there is no proof, DP is the safe route.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Questions four and five: what exactly does the rule sort on, and what is the resulting running time? Name the criterion out loud before computing anything.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Six greedy problems, six criteria: earliest finish, value per weight, two lightest, lightest edge, nearest unfinalised vertex, highest profit first.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Costs: n log n for the three sorting-based ones, E log E for Kruskal, and n squared for the simple slot scan in job sequencing.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Proofs: every one of them is an exchange argument, and each one names the fact that makes the swap safe.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
The rule to carry out of this lesson: greedy is a claim about a criterion, not a technique you apply and hope.

